# Line endings are part of the data here, not a matter of taste.
#
# The stored format requires line feed line endings, and a row points at its
# tabulated block by a digest of that block's bytes. On a clone that converts
# line endings when it writes the working copy, the bytes a digest is computed
# over are not the bytes in the tree, so a digest written on such a clone is
# wrong for every other reader and a digest written elsewhere is refused on it.
# That default is active on one of the platforms this project supports.
#
# `text` normalises to line feed on the way in, so a carriage return cannot
# enter the tree. `eol=lf` writes line feed on the way out whatever the clone is
# configured to do, so the working copy and the blob are the same bytes.
# Neither half is enough alone: without the first a carriage return lands in the
# tree, and without the second it comes back out of a clean tree.

*               text=auto eol=lf

# Named rather than left to the automatic text detection, because these are the
# files whose bytes a digest or a canonical form is a claim about, and detection
# is a guess this project should not be making about its own product.

*.json          text eol=lf
*.tsv           text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.toml          text eol=lf
*.rs            text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf

# What none of this reaches, written here rather than left to be found.
#
# Anything git decides is binary. `text=auto` leaves such a file alone in both
# directions, and a pattern marked `binary` or `-text` later would sit outside
# every line above. Nothing is marked either way today, so nothing is currently
# excluded, and whoever adds the first such pattern owes the reason beside it.
#
# And these lines convert rather than refuse, which is not the same guard. They
# make the bytes right on the way in and on the way out of a clone that honours
# them; they say nothing about a working copy that was written before they
# landed, and such a copy keeps its carriage returns until git touches the file
# again. A file that was fixed on one machine and a file that is right
# everywhere look identical afterwards. The gate is what refuses the difference,
# reading the working copy, skipping what these lines skip and counting what it
# skipped rather than passing over it quietly.
